1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,976
How does the merchant's server learn that a payment is paid?

2
00:00:03,026 --> 00:00:05,664
The shop needs to know before it ships the goods.

3
00:00:05,714 --> 00:00:10,920
Its server might be down for an hour, and still needs every event when it comes back, in order.

4
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:13,968
It must also tell a real event from a forged one.

5
00:00:14,018 --> 00:00:16,752
Anyone can send a message that says paid.

6
00:00:16,802 --> 00:00:18,672
And there is a danger for the bank.

7
00:00:18,722 --> 00:00:25,008
An attacker could set their webhook address to a server inside the bank's own network, and make ecom call it.

8
00:00:25,058 --> 00:00:26,880
Think of a registered letter.

9
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:29,856
It is sealed, so the reader knows who sent it.

10
00:00:29,906 --> 00:00:32,280
It goes only to a real street address.

11
00:00:32,330 --> 00:00:35,448
If nobody is home, the postman comes back later.

12
00:00:35,498 --> 00:00:38,736
And after a while, the letter is marked as returned.

13
00:00:38,786 --> 00:00:40,752
A webhook is that letter.

14
00:00:40,802 --> 00:00:46,680
When the news about a payment leaves the outbox, ecom posts it to the website's webhook address.

15
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:49,896
That address is set by the merchant for each website.

16
00:00:49,946 --> 00:00:55,296
Every event carries its own id, the time it was sent, and a webhook signature.

17
00:00:55,346 --> 00:01:00,408
The signature is made with the same signing key as the merchant's API client.

18
00:01:00,458 --> 00:01:04,872
It follows a common open standard, so any webhook library can check it.

19
00:01:04,922 --> 00:01:11,424
The merchant's server checks the seal, drops events that are too old, and drops any id it has seen before.

20
00:01:11,474 --> 00:01:14,832
Before connecting, ecom checks the address.

21
00:01:14,882 --> 00:01:16,968
It must be a secure address.

22
00:01:17,018 --> 00:01:19,392
It must lead only to the public internet.

23
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:24,096
And it is checked again at the very moment of connecting, so it cannot change in between.

24
00:01:24,146 --> 00:01:26,112
Redirects are not followed.

25
00:01:26,162 --> 00:01:33,024
This guard stops server-side request forgery: a trick where someone makes a server call places it should never reach.

26
00:01:33,074 --> 00:01:37,608
A delivery succeeds only when the merchant's server answers with success.

27
00:01:37,658 --> 00:01:40,920
Anything else is tried again, waiting longer each time.

28
00:01:40,970 --> 00:01:45,480
A payment's later events wait behind an earlier one, so they stay in order.

29
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:50,520
After the bank's chosen time, an event that never got through is marked undelivered.

30
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:52,680
The merchant can then ask for it again.

31
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:57,000
Payment links get no webhooks, because there is no server behind them.

32
00:01:57,050 --> 00:01:58,872
Now watch the product.

33
00:01:58,922 --> 00:02:01,224
In the merchant portal, open a payment.

34
00:02:01,274 --> 00:02:05,184
Below its calls to the card scheme is a section called webhooks.

35
00:02:05,234 --> 00:02:07,800
Each event shows its type and its status.

36
00:02:07,850 --> 00:02:12,864
Here it says delivered, after one attempt, with the last answer from the merchant's server.

37
00:02:12,914 --> 00:02:16,392
Now take the merchant's server offline, and make a new payment.

38
00:02:16,442 --> 00:02:22,008
Its event says retrying, with the time of the next attempt and the last answer, which is no answer.

39
00:02:22,058 --> 00:02:25,896
A later event for the same payment says waiting for an earlier event.

40
00:02:25,946 --> 00:02:30,144
While an event is still retrying, there is no button to send it again.

41
00:02:30,194 --> 00:02:32,568
In the end, it says undelivered.

42
00:02:32,618 --> 00:02:39,360
The hint says the server never answered with success, and asks the merchant to fix it, then send the event again.

43
00:02:39,410 --> 00:02:42,768
The merchant brings the server back, and presses send again.

44
00:02:42,818 --> 00:02:46,560
A short note says it is queued and goes out within seconds.

45
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:48,600
The status turns to delivered.

46
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:56,976
Open a payment made from a link, and the section says no webhook was sent: the website has no webhook address, or it is a payment link.

47
00:02:57,026 --> 00:02:58,848
What does this protect?

48
00:02:58,898 --> 00:03:02,016
The signature protects the merchant from a forged paid.

49
00:03:02,066 --> 00:03:06,096
The retries protect the merchant from losing news during an outage.

50
00:03:06,146 --> 00:03:09,360
The order protects against seeing refunded before paid.

51
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:13,296
And the address guard protects the bank's own network from its own webhooks.

52
00:03:13,346 --> 00:03:15,168
So back to the registered letter.

53
00:03:15,218 --> 00:03:19,944
Sealed, sent only to a real address, and tried again until it arrives.

54
00:03:19,994 --> 00:03:22,368
Next: payments without a website.
